<%# /agents: the page a PERSON reads. The reader is a friend with a free-entry
    email, probably on a phone, possibly not technical. So: one sentence, then
    the thing to copy, then three steps. Everything an agent needs in depth
    lives in the guide (agents/guide_source.text.erb), and this page links
    there instead of repeating it.

    THE PROMPT IS RENDERED ONCE (AgentsController#show) and used twice: the
    block a person reads, and the copy button's payload. Never type it here.
    The Claude Code command is built once the same way (mcp_connect_command).

    TWO WAYS TO PLAY, the prompt first: it works with any assistant that can
    make web requests. The second, connecting Claude Code to the MCP endpoint,
    is for a reader who already has Claude Code.

    NOT ON THIS PAGE, ON PURPOSE: any promise that the Claude chat app will be
    able to connect (it cannot take a personal key for most accounts, and
    nothing that would change that is scheduled), and any statement about what
    the Terms allow an AI agent to do. %>
<% content_for :title, "Play with your AI assistant — Turf Monster" %>
<% content_for :meta_description, "Hand Turf Monster to your AI assistant: copy the starter prompt or connect Claude Code, add your API key, and it can read contests, build a lineup and enter with your free entry." %>

<%# The engine's copy button, restyled from outside: its own button is a small
    text link, too small a target for the one action each block exists for.
    min-h-11 is 44px, the smallest target a thumb reliably hits. The inline
    code chip is hidden because the whole text is shown in the block below. %>
<% copy_button_classes = "shrink-0 [&_code]:hidden [&_button]:min-h-11 [&_button]:min-w-11 [&_button]:rounded-lg [&_button]:border [&_button]:border-strong [&_button]:bg-surface-alt [&_button]:px-4 [&_button]:py-2 [&_button]:text-sm [&_button]:font-semibold [&_button]:text-heading" %>

<div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto py-10 px-2 space-y-12" data-test="agents-page">
  <header>
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-extrabold text-heading mb-3">Play Turf Monster with your AI assistant</h1>
    <p class="text-body text-lg leading-relaxed">
      Copy the prompt below, give it to your assistant with your API key, and it can read the contests, work out a lineup with you and enter using your free entry.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section aria-labelledby="starter-prompt-heading">
    <div class="card p-4 sm:p-6">
      <div class="flex items-center justify-between gap-3 mb-3">
        <h2 id="starter-prompt-heading" class="text-lg font-bold text-heading">Starter prompt</h2>
        <div data-test="starter-prompt-copy" class="<%= copy_button_classes %>">
          <%= render "components/copy_button", text: @starter_prompt, display: "Starter prompt" %>
        </div>
      </div>
      <pre data-test="starter-prompt" class="bg-inset border border-subtle rounded-lg p-3 text-sm text-body font-sans leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-wrap break-words max-h-96 overflow-y-auto"><%= @starter_prompt %></pre>
      <p class="text-secondary text-sm mt-3">
        Replace <span class="font-mono text-heading">[PASTE YOUR API KEY HERE]</span> with your key before you send it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-heading mb-4">Three steps</h2>
    <ol class="space-y-3">
      <li class="card p-5">
        <p class="font-mono text-primary text-sm mb-1">01</p>
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-heading mb-1">Claim your free entry</h3>
        <p class="text-body text-sm leading-relaxed">Open the email we sent you and follow its link. That signs you in and puts a free entry on your account.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="card p-5">
        <p class="font-mono text-primary text-sm mb-1">02</p>
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-heading mb-1">Create an API key</h3>
        <p class="text-body text-sm leading-relaxed">
          On your <%= link_to "account page", account_path, class: "text-primary hover:underline" %>, find <span class="text-heading font-semibold">Agent API keys</span>, give the key a name and create it. It is shown once, so copy it then.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class="card p-5">
        <p class="font-mono text-primary text-sm mb-1">03</p>
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-heading mb-1">Paste the prompt and your key</h3>
        <p class="text-body text-sm leading-relaxed">Paste the starter prompt into your assistant with your key in place. It will show you a lineup and ask before it enters anything.</p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="connect-claude-code-heading">
    <div class="card p-4 sm:p-6">
      <div class="flex items-center justify-between gap-3 mb-3">
        <h2 id="connect-claude-code-heading" class="text-lg font-bold text-heading">Connect Claude Code</h2>
        <div data-test="mcp-command-copy" class="<%= copy_button_classes %>">
          <%= render "components/copy_button", text: mcp_connect_command, display: "Claude Code command" %>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="text-body text-sm leading-relaxed mb-3">
        If you use Claude Code, you can skip the prompt and connect it to Turf Monster directly. Run this once in your terminal, with your key in place of <span class="font-mono text-heading break-all"><%= AgentsController::KEY_PLACEHOLDER %></span>:
      </p>
      <pre data-test="mcp-command" class="bg-inset border border-subtle rounded-lg p-3 text-sm text-body font-mono leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-wrap break-all"><%= mcp_connect_command %></pre>
      <p class="text-body text-sm leading-relaxed mt-3">
        Then open Claude Code in the same folder and say: <span class="text-heading">&ldquo;Play Turf Monster for me. Check my account, show me the open contests and suggest a lineup. Ask me before you enter anything.&rdquo;</span>
      </p>
      <p class="text-secondary text-sm leading-relaxed mt-3">
        The command saves your key in Claude Code's own settings on your computer. To take it out again, run <span class="font-mono text-heading break-words">claude mcp remove <%= AgentMcp::Server::SERVER_INFO.fetch(:name) %></span>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-3">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-heading">Which assistants can do this</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-5 text-body space-y-2 leading-relaxed" data-test="agents-clients">
      <li><span class="text-heading font-semibold">Claude Code</span> works today, either way: with the starter prompt, or connected with the command above.</li>
      <li><span class="text-heading font-semibold">Codex, or any other assistant that can make web requests on its own,</span> works with the starter prompt.</li>
      <li><span class="text-heading font-semibold">The Claude chat app</span> (claude.ai and the desktop and phone apps) does not work for most people today. A chat cannot make web requests on its own, so the prompt does nothing there. It can connect to outside services, but for most accounts it has no place to put a personal key: Anthropic describes that option as a beta open to a limited set of organizations. If you use Claude in chat, use Claude Code for this instead.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-3">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-heading">What your key can and cannot do</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-5 text-body space-y-2 leading-relaxed">
      <li>It lets your assistant read contests and enter one for you, paid for with your free entry.</li>
      <li>It can pay an entry fee from your USDC, but only when a request explicitly asks for that. The starter prompt tells your assistant not to ask unless you say so.</li>
      <li>It stops working after <%= (ApiKey::LIFETIME / 1.day).to_i %> days.</li>
      <li>You can revoke it at any time from your <%= link_to "account page", account_path, class: "text-primary hover:underline" %>, and it stops working at once.</li>
      <li>Treat it like a password. Anyone holding it can enter contests as you.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="text-secondary text-sm leading-relaxed">
      You are responsible for what your assistant does on your account. See the <%= link_to "Terms of Service", terms_path, class: "text-primary hover:underline" %>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-heading mb-2">The API in brief</h2>
    <p class="text-body leading-relaxed mb-4">
      Every request goes to <span class="font-mono text-heading break-words"><%= agent_base_url %>/api/v1</span> with the header <span class="font-mono text-heading break-words">Authorization: Bearer &lt;your key&gt;</span>.
    </p>
    <%# Two columns from the sm breakpoint up. Narrower than that, a request
        line that may not wrap left the second column about 27px wide, so each
        row STACKS: the request on one line, what it does underneath. The
        header row would label nothing in that layout and is kept for screen
        readers only. e2e/agents_pages.spec.js measures the second cell. %>
    <div class="card">
      <table class="w-full text-sm block sm:table" data-test="agents-endpoints">
        <thead class="sr-only sm:not-sr-only sm:table-header-group">
          <tr class="border-b border-subtle">
            <th class="px-3 py-2 text-left text-xs font-semibold text-muted uppercase tracking-wider">Request</th>
            <th class="px-3 py-2 text-left text-xs font-semibold text-muted uppercase tracking-wider">What it does</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-subtle block sm:table-row-group">
          <% [
               [ "GET /api/v1/me", "Check the key and your free entries" ],
               [ "GET /api/v1/contests", "List contests" ],
               [ "GET /api/v1/contests/:slug", "One contest and the teams to pick from" ],
               [ "GET /api/v1/contests/:slug/leaderboard", "The standings" ],
               [ "GET /api/v1/entries", "Your entries" ],
               [ "POST /api/v1/contests/:slug/entries", "Enter a contest" ],
               [ "PATCH /api/v1/entries/:slug", "Change an entry's teams before the lock" ]
             ].each do |request_line, purpose| %>
            <tr class="block py-2 sm:table-row sm:py-0">
              <td class="block px-3 py-0.5 font-mono text-xs text-heading break-words sm:table-cell sm:py-2 sm:whitespace-nowrap"><%= request_line %></td>
              <td class="block px-3 py-0.5 text-body sm:table-cell sm:py-2" data-test="agents-endpoint-purpose"><%= purpose %></td>
            </tr>
          <% end %>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="card p-5 sm:p-6">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-heading mb-2">The agent guide</h2>
    <p class="text-body leading-relaxed mb-4">
      The full rules, every endpoint and error, the Claude Code connection in detail, and how to reason about a lineup. Your assistant reads it first; you can too.
    </p>
    <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-5 gap-y-3">
      <%= link_to "Read the agent guide", agents_guide_path, class: "btn btn-primary" %>
      <%= link_to "Plain Markdown version", agents_guide_markdown_path, class: "text-primary hover:underline text-sm" %>
    </div>
  </section>
</div>
